Restart the LRM on a node while the CRM defers the disarm because another
service is in a transient state (fence here, in production typically migrate
or relocate).

The LRM enters 'restart' mode and must release its lock and watchdog before
the new LRM instance can come up. That requires active_service_count to drop
to zero, which in turn requires the CRM to freeze the LRM's services. If the
CRM defers the freeze until the transient transition resolves, the LRM has
to wait too. In production the deferred transition often never resolves (a
migrate worker whose LRM was killed mid-flight leaves manager_status stuck
in 'migrate' with no result), so the LRM hangs forever in systemd's
'deactivating (stop)' until SIGKILL, which orphans more workers.

The simulator's fence flow always resolves, so the LRM does eventually exit
even without the fix - just much later than it should.
